          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a urinary tract infection, and why are they more common in women?",
          "answer": "A urinary tract infection (UTI) is a bacterial infection of the urinary system. Most involve the bladder and urethra, the tube that carries urine out of the body. This is called a lower tract infection, or cystitis. *Escherichia coli* causes most of them. (Source: General clinical reference, \"Urinary tract infection.\")\n\nIf the infection reaches the kidney, it is called pyelonephritis. The guidance describes this as a more serious illness, not just a worse form of the same one. (Same source.)\n\nThe guidance separates \"uncomplicated\" infections, which occur in non-pregnant adults with a normal urinary tract and normal immune function, from \"complicated\" ones. Complicated cases include infections in pregnancy, in men, and in people with catheters, kidney stones, structural problems, poorly controlled diabetes, or weakened immunity. (Same source, and \"Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence.\")\n\nI could not find an answer to your second question, why UTIs are more common in women, in the passages I retrieved. I have not filled that gap from memory. A clinician or a more detailed medical reference would be the best place to ask.\n\nIf you have a fever, back or flank pain, vomiting, confusion, or feel very unwell, seek medical care promptly. Guidance on serious infection (sepsis) notes that signs of a serious response to infection can be easy to miss, and that the urinary tract is one common source. (Source: \"Sepsis: signs of a life-threatening response to infection.\")\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
